.TH "checkmake" "1" "January 10, 2026" "checkmake User Manuals" "checkmake User Manuals"
.SH NAME
\f[B]checkmake\f[R] \[en] linter for Makefiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]checkmake\f[R] [options] makefile \&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]checkmake\f[R] is a linter for Makefiles.
It allows for a set of configurable rules being run against a Makefile
or a set of \f[CR]\[rs]*.mk\f[R] files.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
Show this help message and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]version\f[R]
Show version information.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]debug\f[R]
Enable debug output for troubleshooting.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]config\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
Specify the configuration file to read (default:
\f[CR]checkmake.ini\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]format\f[R] \f[I]format\f[R]
Set a custom output format using Go\[cq]s \f[CR]text/template\f[R]
syntax.
This option customizes how violations are displayed in \f[B]text
mode\f[R].
Cannot be used together with \f[B]\[en]output\f[R] (mutually exclusive).
.RS
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
checkmake \-\-format \[aq]{{.Rule}}: {{.Violation}}\[aq] Makefile
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-o\f[R], \f[B]\[en]output\f[R] \f[I]mode\f[R]
Select the overall output mode.
Supported values:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]text\f[R] (default): human\-readable table or formatted text
output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]json\f[R]: structured machine\-readable JSON output.
.PP
When \f[B]\[en]output=json\f[R] is specified, \f[B]\[en]format\f[R] is
ignored and violations are printed as a JSON array.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
checkmake \-o json Makefile | jq
.EE
.RE
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
\f[B]list\-rules\f[R]
Display all registered rules and their descriptions.
.SH RULES
.TP
\f[B]maxbodylength\f[R]
Target bodies should be kept simple and short (no more than 8 lines by
default).
This is number is configurable (see below).
.TP
\f[B]minphony\f[R]
A minimum list of required phony targets must be present By default
these are all,clean,and test.
This list is configurable (see below).
.TP
\f[B]phonydeclared\f[R]
Every target without a body needs to be marked PHONY
.TP
\f[B]timestampexpanded\f[R]
timestamp variables should be simply expanded
.TP
\f[B]uniquetargets\f[R]
Targets should be uniquely defined because duplicates can cause recipe
overrides or unintended merges.
.SH CONFIGURATION
By default checkmake looks for a \f[CR]checkmake.ini\f[R] file in the
same folder it\[cq]s executed in, and then as fallback in
\f[CR]\[ti]/checkmake.ini\f[R].
This can be overridden by passing the \f[CR]\-\-config=\f[R] argument
pointing it to a different configuration file.
With the configuration file the \f[CR][default]\f[R] section is for
checkmake itself while sections named after the rule names are passed to
the rules as their configuration.
All keys/values are hereby treated as strings and passed to the rule in
a string/string map.
.PP
The following configuration options for checkmake itself are supported
within the \f[CR]default\f[R] section:
.TP
\f[B]default.format\f[R]
This enables the custom output formatter with the given template string
as a format
.PP
maxBodylength.maxBodylength This allows to override the maximum number
of lines for a rule body that checkmake will allow from the default of 5
to a different number
.PP
minphony.required This allows to override the list of minimum required
phony targets from the default of (all, test, clean) to any list of
target name strings.
The value is a comma\-separated list of strings.
Setting minphony.required to the empty string disabled the minphony rule
altogether.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\f[CR]checkmake\f[R] exits with the following status codes:
.IP
.EX
 0:   checkmake ran successfully and found no rule violations
 1:   checkmake found one or more rule violations, or encountered an execution error
.EE
.PP
Unlike previous versions, \f[CR]checkmake\f[R] no longer exits with the
exact number of violations.
Any nonzero exit status now indicates that either violations were
detected or an error occurred during execution.
